---
import CalculatorLayout from '@layouts/CalculatorLayout.astro';
import VolumeCalculator from '@components/islands/VolumeCalculator.astro';
import { getCalculator } from '@data/calculators';
const calc = getCalculator('math', 'volume-calculator')!;
const faqs = [
  { question: 'How do I calculate volume?', answer: 'Pick the 3D shape and enter its dimensions. A box is length × width × height; a sphere is 4⁄3 × π × radius³; a cylinder is π × radius² × height. The calculator uses the correct formula for each shape.' },
  { question: 'How do I find the volume of a cylinder?', answer: 'Volume = π × radius² × height. A cylinder with radius 2 and height 5 has a volume of π × 4 × 5 ≈ 62.83 cubic units. It is simply the circular base area (π × r²) multiplied by the height.' },
  { question: 'How do I find the volume of a sphere?', answer: 'Volume = 4⁄3 × π × radius³. A sphere of radius 3 has a volume of 4⁄3 × π × 27 ≈ 113.10 cubic units. Only the radius matters — a sphere has no other dimension.' },
  { question: 'How do I find the volume of a cone?', answer: 'Volume = ⅓ × π × radius² × height — exactly one third of a cylinder with the same base and height. A cone with radius 3 and height 6 holds ⅓ × π × 9 × 6 ≈ 56.55 cubic units.' },
  { question: 'How do I find the volume of a rectangular prism?', answer: 'A rectangular prism (a box) is length × width × height. A box 2 × 3 × 4 has a volume of 24 cubic units. Use the "box" shape above and enter the three dimensions.' },
  { question: 'What units is the volume in?', answer: 'Enter all dimensions in the same unit; the volume is in that unit cubed (e.g. cubic metres or cubic feet). Choose a unit label to match your measurements.' },
];
---
<CalculatorLayout calculator={calc} presentation="task-first" intro="Calculate the volume of a cube, box, sphere, cylinder, cone, pyramid or capsule — select the shape and enter its dimensions." faqs={faqs} reviewMetadata={{ reviewedDate: '2026-07-25' }}>
  <VolumeCalculator />
  <Fragment slot="content">
    <h2>How to use this calculator</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Select a <strong>3D shape</strong> — cube, box, sphere, cylinder, cone, pyramid or capsule.</li>
      <li>Enter the dimensions it asks for, all in the <strong>same unit</strong>.</li>
      <li>Read the volume, which comes out in that unit <strong>cubed</strong> (cubic metres, cubic feet, and so on).</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Volume formulas for every shape</h2>
    <p>Volume is always in cubic units. Each shape has its own formula — these are the seven the calculator solves:</p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Shape</th><th>Formula</th><th>Example</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Cube</td><td>side<sup>3</sup></td><td>3<sup>3</sup> = 27</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Box (rectangular prism)</td><td>length × width × height</td><td>2 × 3 × 4 = 24</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Sphere</td><td>4⁄3 × π × radius<sup>3</sup></td><td>4⁄3 × π × 3<sup>3</sup> ≈ 113.10</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Cylinder</td><td>π × radius<sup>2</sup> × height</td><td>π × 2<sup>2</sup> × 5 ≈ 62.83</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Cone</td><td>⅓ × π × radius<sup>2</sup> × height</td><td>⅓ × π × 3<sup>2</sup> × 6 ≈ 56.55</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Pyramid</td><td>⅓ × base length × base width × height</td><td>⅓ × 4 × 4 × 9 = 48</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Capsule</td><td>π × r<sup>2</sup> × (4⁄3 × r + height)</td><td>π × 2<sup>2</sup> × (4⁄3 × 2 + 5) ≈ 96.34</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>A worked example</h2>
    <p>
      Find the volume of a ball with a <strong>radius of 3</strong>. The sphere formula is 4⁄3 × π × radius<sup>3</sup> = 4⁄3 × π × 3<sup>3</sup> = 4⁄3 × π × 27 = 36π ≈ <strong>113.10 cubic units</strong>. A cylinder with a <strong>radius of 2</strong> and a <strong>height of 5</strong> is π × radius<sup>2</sup> × height = π × 4 × 5 = 20π ≈ <strong>62.83 cubic units</strong> — simply its circular base area multiplied by how tall it is.
    </p>

    <h2>The one-third pattern</h2>
    <p>
      Two of these formulas share a neat relationship. A <strong>cone</strong> holds exactly one third of the cylinder that would enclose it, and a <strong>pyramid</strong> holds one third of its enclosing box — both have the same base and height as the fuller shape. That is why the cone and pyramid formulas are just the cylinder and box formulas with a ⅓ in front.
    </p>

    <h2>How the capsule is built</h2>
    <p>
      A capsule is a cylinder with a hemisphere capped on each end — a pill shape. Its volume is the cylinder plus a full sphere (the two hemispheres together), which is why the formula reads π × r<sup>2</sup> × (4⁄3 × r + height). Here the <strong>height</strong> is only the straight cylindrical section, not the rounded caps. With a radius of 2 and a cylinder height of 5, that is π × 4 × (4⁄3 × 2 + 5) = π × 4 × 7.667 ≈ <strong>96.34 cubic units</strong>.
    </p>

    <h2>Keep your units consistent</h2>
    <p>
      Because volume multiplies three lengths, the units cube: metres give cubic metres, feet give cubic feet. Convert every dimension to one unit before you calculate. If you need capacity rather than raw volume, remember the standard conversions — one cubic metre is 1,000 litres, and one cubic foot is about 7.48 US gallons.
    </p>

    <h2>Everyday uses</h2>
    <p>
      Volume calculations help with shipping and packaging, filling a pool or tank, cooking, and construction. For pouring a concrete slab, the <a href="/everyday/concrete-calculator">concrete calculator</a> converts volume into cubic yards and bags. For flat areas rather than solids, see the <a href="/math/area-calculator">area calculator</a>.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</CalculatorLayout>
